  glossary: {
    kicker: 'Speak the language',
    title: 'AI glossary',
    text: 'The terms you’ll run into in prompting and agents, explained in plain words.',
    searchLabel: 'Search the glossary',
    placeholder: n => `Search ${n} terms…`,
    empty: q => `Nothing found for “${q}”.`,
    items: [
      { t: 'Agent', d: 'A system where an LLM, in a loop, chooses and calls tools on its own until it meets a goal. It doesn’t just talk — it acts.' },
      { t: 'Chain-of-thought', d: 'A chain of reasoning while solving a task. Reasoning models think internally; ask for a brief justification and a check of the result.' },
      { t: 'Embedding', d: 'Turning text into a vector that captures its meaning. The basis of search by meaning and of RAG.' },
      { t: 'Evaluation (eval)', d: 'Measuring quality on a set of real tasks with criteria set in advance. Helps compare prompts and models without relying on one lucky output.' },
      { t: 'Few-shot', d: 'A few input→output examples right in the prompt. The model copies a pattern better than a verbal description.' },
      { t: 'Fine-tuning', d: 'Extra training for a specific task or style. Needs quality data and measurement; it doesn’t replace searching for current facts.' },
      { t: 'Hallucination', d: 'A confident but false statement. The model generates likely text, not verified truth.' },
      { t: 'Human-in-the-loop', d: 'An architecture where a person approves an agent’s critical or irreversible steps.' },
      { t: 'Context window', d: 'The maximum number of tokens a model can see at once. Anything over the limit drops out — which is why long chats lose the thread.' },
      { t: 'MCP', d: 'Model Context Protocol — a standard for connecting tools and data to models. “USB” for AI.' },
      { t: 'Multimodality', d: 'Working with more than one kind of input or output, such as text and images. Supported formats vary by model and app.' },
      { t: 'Tool', d: 'A function the model can call: search, code, an API. It extends the model with actions.' },
      { t: 'Orchestrator', d: 'A lead agent that splits work among sub-agents and combines the results.' },
      { t: 'Prompt', d: 'The input to a model — instructions, a question, context. The result also depends on the model, the material, and the tools available.' },
      { t: 'Prompt injection', d: 'Instructions hidden in untrusted material that try to change an agent’s behavior. Keep data separate from instructions and limit tool permissions.' },
      { t: 'RAG', d: 'Retrieval-Augmented Generation: before answering, the model looks up documents and bases its answer on them.' },
      { t: 'ReAct', d: 'Reasoning + Acting: a pattern where the model alternates between thinking and tool actions. The backbone of agents.' },
      { t: 'Reasoning model', d: 'A model that spends compute on internal reasoning. Higher effort can help with hard tasks; expect slower responses and possibly higher costs.' },
      { t: 'Self-consistency', d: 'Generating several solutions and comparing how well they agree. It can help, but even matching answers from several models can still be wrong.' },
      { t: 'System prompt', d: 'A persistent instruction above the conversation: role, tone, rules. It applies to the whole chat.' },
      { t: 'Temperature', d: 'A setting for output randomness. A low value doesn’t guarantee accuracy. Some reasoning models don’t support it.' },
      { t: 'Token', d: 'The unit input is split into. The ratio to words depends on the language and the model; there’s no fixed conversion.' },
    ],
  },

  faq: {
    kicker: 'FAQ',
    title: 'Common questions',
    items: [
      { q: 'Do I need to code to use agents?', a: 'Not to understand the principles. For building your own agents, the basics (Python, APIs) help, but many no-code tools now let you build a working agentic workflow without code. Start by understanding the loop and the architectures — the tool comes second.' },
      { q: 'What’s the difference between a chatbot and an agent?', a: 'A chatbot answers a message and waits. An agent gets a goal and, in a loop, decides, calls tools, and repeats steps until the goal is met — without you dictating each step. The key is the autonomous loop and access to tools.' },
      { q: 'Do even the newest models hallucinate?', a: 'Yes. How often depends on the model, the task, and the available material. Hallucination comes from how models work — they generate likely text, not verified truth. So ask for sources and confidence on facts, check critical figures, and use RAG or search.' },
      { q: 'Is it worth paying for premium models?', a: 'For simple tasks, cheaper or free models are often enough. For harder reasoning, code, long context, and agent loops, a stronger model can save iterations. Test it on your own tasks.' },
      { q: 'How long should the ideal prompt be?', a: 'Long enough to include the context and instructions it needs — and not a word more. A structured prompt with five clear parts beats a paragraph of fluff. Length isn’t the goal; clarity is.' },
      { q: 'What’s the most common beginner mistake?', a: 'Zero context and a vague brief — people write to the model as if it could see inside their head. The second: settling for the first answer instead of iterating.' },
      { q: 'When do I need an agent rather than a prompt?', a: 'When the task needs several steps, current data, or actions in the world, and you’d otherwise copy outputs between prompts by hand. If one good prompt or a short chain handles it, you don’t need an agent.' },
      { q: 'Is my data in a prompt safe?', a: 'It depends on the provider and settings. Don’t put sensitive data (passwords, ID numbers, medical records) into public tools without a clear guarantee. For work, look for a mode that doesn’t train on your data and follow data protection rules such as GDPR. When in doubt, anonymize.' },
    ],
  },

